// Tier A (sandboxed WASM Component Model) plugin interface -- first slice.
//
// This is deliberately minimal: it proves the *mechanism* (a component
// lifecycle, and one capability-gated host call reaching real
// canary-ecs::World state) rather than attempting the full ECS
// read/write data ABI described in docs/roadmap/v0.0.3-roadmap.md
// (SCHEMA_ID-addressed get/set/has-component/is-valid-entity over a
// capped value-type set). That remains its own, larger piece of work --
// see engine/canary-plugin-api/src/tier_a.rs's module docs for exactly
// what this slice does and does not cover.
//
// `name` is deliberately not part of this interface: a WIT function
// returning `string` needs canonical-ABI memory (a guest-exported
// `memory` and `realloc`) that adds real complexity without validating
// anything this slice is actually trying to prove. A Tier A plugin's
// name is supplied by its loader's caller for now -- see
// `WasmPluginLoader::load`'s doc comment. Revisit once there's a real
// reason to (e.g. a plugin manifest, R-08).

package canary:plugin@0.1.0;

interface lifecycle {
    on-load: func();
    on-unload: func();
    // Testing/illustration hook: exposes what `on-load` cached from the
    // `ecs-read` import below, so a host-side test can observe that a
    // value genuinely round-tripped through a real component instance
    // rather than asserting on the host's own state.
    last-entity-count: func() -> u32;
}

// One capability's worth of the eventual "read ECS state" surface --
// see the module docs above for why this isn't the full thing yet.
interface ecs-read {
    entity-count: func() -> u32;
}

world tier-a-plugin {
    export lifecycle;
    import ecs-read;
}
